WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.000
-Our next guest is the host
of the Colum Tyrrell Podcast

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.501
available on YouTube.

00:00:04.501 --> 00:00:06.000
Making his television debut,

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.567
please welcome the very funny
Colum Tyrrell.

00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:11.567
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:11.567 --> 00:00:16.901
♪♪

00:00:16.901 --> 00:00:19.033
-Hi. How's it going?

00:00:19.033 --> 00:00:20.901
[ Cheers ]

00:00:20.901 --> 00:00:24.234
I'm -- I'm from Ireland, okay?

00:00:24.234 --> 00:00:26.467
So I'm not as ugly as I look.

00:00:26.467 --> 00:00:28.267
[ Laughter ]

00:00:28.267 --> 00:00:29.734
People see me, they go, "Oh."

00:00:29.734 --> 00:00:31.467
And then they hear me speak,
and they go,

00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:35.934
"Actually, for an Irish guy,
not too bad."

00:00:35.934 --> 00:00:38.000
[ Light laughter ]

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.267
You know "Game of Thrones?"

00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:41.501
They filmed that in Ireland.

00:00:41.501 --> 00:00:44.367
They did that to save on make-up
costs with the extras.

00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:47.033
[ Laughter ]

00:00:47.033 --> 00:00:48.534
The filming crew showed up
and were like,

00:00:48.534 --> 00:00:50.467
"Hey, street merchant
with leprosy,

00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:52.400
we're ready for your scene.
Come on."

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.067
[ Light laughter ]

00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:57.601
That guy was like,
"No, I work with catering."

00:00:57.601 --> 00:01:01.434
And they said, "Get in here,
you ugly pig, we need you."

00:01:01.434 --> 00:01:03.701
Bring all your friends
and your family

00:01:03.701 --> 00:01:05.634
because we need some wildings
for free."

00:01:05.634 --> 00:01:07.501
[ Laughter ]

00:01:07.501 --> 00:01:10.067
If you rewatch that show
and look in the background,

00:01:10.067 --> 00:01:12.667
you'll see a bunch
of Irish plumbers and stuff,

00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:14.534
staring at the camera.

00:01:14.534 --> 00:01:17.534
[ Laughter ]

00:01:19.334 --> 00:01:21.634
Every time a dragon flies by,
they waved.

00:01:21.634 --> 00:01:24.634
[ Laughter ]

00:01:26.701 --> 00:01:28.067
I love ugly people.

00:01:28.067 --> 00:01:29.367
[ Laughter ]

00:01:29.367 --> 00:01:30.901
I was a waiter for years.

00:01:30.901 --> 00:01:34.067
I never had an ugly person
complain about anything.

00:01:34.067 --> 00:01:35.734
It's always
a good-looking person who wants

00:01:35.734 --> 00:01:37.834
to speak to the manager,

00:01:37.834 --> 00:01:39.868
usually a beautiful woman.

00:01:39.868 --> 00:01:43.167
She would be like, "I ordered
my steak medium rare

00:01:43.167 --> 00:01:46.634
and this is medium-medium-Rare."

00:01:46.634 --> 00:01:48.434
[ Laughter ]

00:01:48.434 --> 00:01:50.601
Ugly people,
they never complain.

00:01:50.601 --> 00:01:52.767
They're just happy to be out
of the house.

00:01:52.767 --> 00:01:54.734
[ Laughter ]

00:01:54.734 --> 00:01:56.634
They ordered a steak
medium rare.

00:01:56.634 --> 00:02:00.667
You can just throw a fish
at their head.

00:02:00.667 --> 00:02:03.634
You wouldn't even need to kill
the fish.

00:02:03.634 --> 00:02:07.167
Just lob it towards their ugly
face, and they'll catch it

00:02:07.167 --> 00:02:09.868
in their fang like, hyah!

00:02:09.868 --> 00:02:12.467
And then they break its neck.

00:02:12.467 --> 00:02:14.901
And they'll smile at you
as fish guts dribble down

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:17.100
their ugly chin
onto their ugly body

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:19.167
because they're happy
for the eye contact.

00:02:19.167 --> 00:02:21.067
[ Laughter ]

00:02:21.067 --> 00:02:24.467
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:24.467 --> 00:02:27.901
I love -- I love ugly people.

00:02:27.901 --> 00:02:30.033
I want ugly kids

00:02:30.033 --> 00:02:33.200
because you don't have to parent
ugly children.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.267
Life will teach them
their lessons naturally.

00:02:37.267 --> 00:02:40.267
[ Laughter ]

00:02:43.734 --> 00:02:46.000
I'm dating an American girl.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.634
We've been together for 5 years
now, so we're almost done.

00:02:49.634 --> 00:02:50.567
[ Laughter ]

00:02:50.567 --> 00:02:54.300
Yeah. [ Laughs ]

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.167
Ah, she's all right.

00:02:56.167 --> 00:02:57.267
She's okay.

00:02:57.267 --> 00:02:59.300
She's into the environment
though,

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.334
and she recently bought
some coffee cups

00:03:01.334 --> 00:03:03.968
that say "Save the Bees."

00:03:03.968 --> 00:03:10.200
The bees, guys -- oh, God,
the bees are -- they're dying.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.434
[ Laughter ]

00:03:11.434 --> 00:03:13.801
And you can't go outside of
your house anymore

00:03:13.801 --> 00:03:16.734
without piles of
dead bees everywhere you go.

00:03:16.734 --> 00:03:21.334
And if the bees die, there'll be
no more Internet or something.

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:23.200
I don't know.
I zone out when she talks.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.901
But it's important.
It's -- I'm told it's important.

00:03:25.901 --> 00:03:28.467
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:03:28.467 --> 00:03:31.501
So, every morning, we drink
coffee from these coffee cups

00:03:31.501 --> 00:03:33.334
that say "Save the Bees,"
alright?

00:03:33.334 --> 00:03:36.200
I don't know what you guys are
doing, but we're helping.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:39.701
[ Laughter ]

00:03:39.701 --> 00:03:44.534
Recently -- recently, there was
a dead bee on our windowsill,

00:03:44.534 --> 00:03:47.167
and my girlfriend is like,
"We have to save the bee."

00:03:47.167 --> 00:03:50.567
And I go, "Yeah,
but he's already dead.

00:03:50.567 --> 00:03:52.667
He must not have seen
the coffee cups

00:03:52.667 --> 00:03:58.033
because that is a dead bee
if ever I saw one."

00:03:58.033 --> 00:04:01.667
But apparently -- she explained
to me, apparently,

00:04:01.667 --> 00:04:04.033
a lot of the time when you see
a dead bee,

00:04:04.033 --> 00:04:06.334
apparently it's
not actually dead.

00:04:06.334 --> 00:04:10.100
Most of the time, it's actually
just really thirsty

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:13.701
and he's having a little
dehydrated nap.

00:04:13.701 --> 00:04:14.968
And it will die,

00:04:14.968 --> 00:04:18.133
but you can save its life
by giving it a drink.

00:04:18.133 --> 00:04:21.734
This is the type of ridiculous
stuff that comes out of her face

00:04:21.734 --> 00:04:24.267
all of the time.

00:04:24.267 --> 00:04:26.734
But I can't leave her
because I need a visa.

00:04:26.734 --> 00:04:29.734
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:04:33.634 --> 00:04:37.934
So, I prepared -- I prepared
a dead bee drink

00:04:37.934 --> 00:04:39.534
because I love this country.

00:04:39.534 --> 00:04:42.901
[ Laughter ]

00:04:42.901 --> 00:04:44.701
And I got tap water.

00:04:44.701 --> 00:04:48.767
And she goes, "Not tap water.
They hate tap water.

00:04:48.767 --> 00:04:51.200
They only like sugary water."

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:54.000
What are the odds of getting
such a picky dead bee?

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.667
[ Laughter ]

00:04:55.667 --> 00:04:58.033
You give it tap water,
he turns into Gordon Ramsay.

00:04:58.033 --> 00:05:03.400
Just, "[Spits] Ah! it's bland!

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:08.968
This water is bland!"

00:05:08.968 --> 00:05:12.300
So, I mixed sugar with water
to make simple syrup.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:15.767
I'm a mixologist now
for a dead bee.

00:05:15.767 --> 00:05:18.968
And I put the sugary water
on a spoon.

00:05:18.968 --> 00:05:20.968
But it won't drink it.

00:05:20.968 --> 00:05:24.634
This dead bee has lost
its thirst.

00:05:24.634 --> 00:05:28.367
So, I just start splashing
its corpse,

00:05:28.367 --> 00:05:32.133
just covering its dead body
in a sticky liquid,

00:05:32.133 --> 00:05:34.934
dishonoring its memory
completely.

00:05:34.934 --> 00:05:37.334
And the whole time, my
girlfriend is over my shoulder

00:05:37.334 --> 00:05:38.968
like, "Is it working?"

00:05:38.968 --> 00:05:41.934
And I go,
"No, it's not working."

00:05:41.934 --> 00:05:43.067
And she goes, "Why not?"

00:05:43.067 --> 00:05:46.434
I go, "Because it's dead.
It's a dead bee."

00:05:46.434 --> 00:05:47.667
And I know it was dead

00:05:47.667 --> 00:05:50.200
because I had killed it
with a magazine

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:52.200
earlier that day...

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:53.467
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:53.467 --> 00:05:56.667
when he came
into my home uninvited.

00:05:56.667 --> 00:05:57.868
Thank you very much.

00:05:57.868 --> 00:05:59.133
Appreciate it!
Thank you!

00:05:59.133 --> 00:06:00.834
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:00.834 --> 00:06:03.400
-That's how you do it.
That's how you do it, brother.

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:05.234
Fantastic.

00:06:05.234 --> 00:06:07.868
Colum Tyrrell, everybody.

00:06:07.868 --> 00:06:10.801
His podcast is available
on YouTube.

00:06:10.801 --> 00:06:12.634
We'll be right back, everybody.
Come on back.

